\description{
Once the surface stores have emptied after rain, the flow is the groundwater store
draining, and how fast it falls for a given flow depends on the store's form. Plotted on
logarithmic axes, the rate of fall \code{-dQ/dt} against the flow \code{Q} lies along a line whose
slope identifies the store (Brutsaert and Nieber, 1977; Kirchner, 2009):
}
\details{
\tabular{lll}{
   Store \tab Outflow \tab Slope \cr
   linear \tab q = S / kb \tab 1 \cr
   quadratic \tab q = S^2 / kb \tab 1.5 \cr
   cubic \tab q = S^3 / kb \tab 1.67 \cr
   exponential \tab q = exp(a S + gamma) \tab 2 \cr
}


A power store q = k S^m has slope (2m - 1) / m, so a measured slope b also gives the
exponent m = 1 / (2 - b) of \code{\link[=power_routing]{power_routing()}}.

\strong{Which points count.} The flow is averaged over \code{aggregate_h} hours (daily by default:
at 15-minute steps the rate of fall is mostly noise). A step counts when the flow falls,
no rain above \code{rain_tol} fell in it, and at least \code{dry_h} hours have passed since the last
rain, so that the surface stores have drained; by default three response times of the
catchment (\code{\link[=response_time_dmca]{response_time_dmca()}}), or \code{aggregate_h} where none can be found. Runs of
falling steps shorter than \code{min_steps} are left out, and the first \code{skip_steps} of each run.
\code{months} keeps only recessions in those months: evaporation steepens summer recessions, so
winter ones show the store more cleanly.

\strong{The fit.} The points are binned by flow on a log scale, and a straight line is fitted to
the logs of the bins' mean flow and mean rate, weighted by their counts, as Kirchner (2009)
recommends; bins with fewer than three points are dropped. The confidence interval comes
from refitting on \code{boot} resamples of whole recession runs, which keeps the dependence
within a run. Every store whose slope lies inside the interval is consistent with the
record. Where several are, the record cannot choose between them, and that is the answer.

\strong{Recharge between events flattens the slope.} The table holds for a store that only
drains. With standard recharge, the soil store keeps feeding groundwater through every
recession, and the flow falls more slowly than the store alone would let it. On made-up
catchments with ten years of hourly rain, with split recharge (none between events) the
fitted slopes were 0.98, 1.50, 1.63 and 1.92 for the four stores, close to the table; with
standard recharge they were 0.1 to 0.9 lower, by an amount depending on the store and the
recharge rate, and the stores ran together. Fitting the steepest points instead did not
remove the bias.
So read a store straight from the slope only where recharge stops between events.

\strong{Choosing a store.} In a benchmark of sixteen such catchments, each calibrated with all four
stores (\code{inst/benchmarks/recession_stores.R}), the slope read from the record found the true
store in 8, and the fitted store whose simulated slope came closest in 9: each fitted store
bends its parameters until its recessions look roughly right. The best validation score found
it in all 16, but by a median of 0.003 in log NSE. Use this analysis to check that a fitted
model's low flows fall at the right rate (\code{\link[=plot_recession]{plot_recession()}} draws the record and the model
together), not to choose its store; see the calibration study article.
}
\examples{
set.seed(2)
n <- 24 * 365 * 3
forcing <- data.frame(rain = ifelse(runif(n) < 0.03, rgamma(n, 0.7, scale = 6), 0),
